<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="cfr-classical">
  <title>Classical rationality constructions</title>
  <section xml:id="cfr-rationality">
    <title>Rationality and the dimension four problem</title>
    <p>Throughout these notes, a cubic fourfold is a smooth hypersurface <m>X\subset\mathbb P^5_{\mathbb C}</m> of degree three. It is rational if there is a birational map <m>\mathbb P^4\dashrightarrow X</m>, stably rational if <m>X\times\mathbb P^r</m> is rational for some <m>r\geq0</m>, and unirational if it admits a dominant rational map from a projective space. These implications become weaker in that order. A general member of an irreducible family lies in a specified nonempty Zariski open subset. A very general member lies outside a specified countable union of proper closed algebraic subsets. The distinction matters when a statement excludes infinitely many loci of additional Hodge classes.</p>
    <p>Smooth complex cubic hypersurfaces of positive dimension at least two are unirational. For a cubic fourfold, the construction using a line gives a parametrization of degree two, as described in <xref ref="bib-cfr-hassett-survey" detail="Proposition 10"/>. Degree two does not give a birational map. Rationality constructions must remove the extra choice responsible for that degree, usually by imposing a surface inside the cubic.</p>
    <p>A singular cubic with an ordinary double point is rational by projection from that point. After placing the point at <m>[1:0:\cdots:0]</m>, its equation has the form <m>x_0q_2(x_1,\ldots,x_5)+q_3(x_1,\ldots,x_5)=0</m>. Away from <m>q_2=0</m>, the projected coordinates determine <m>x_0</m>. These singular examples are distinct from the smooth cubics considered in the rationality problem.</p>
    <p>Clemens and Griffiths proved that every smooth cubic threefold is irrational by showing that its principally polarized intermediate Jacobian is not a product of Jacobians of curves <xref ref="bib-cfr-clemens-griffiths" detail="Section 13"/>. Blow-ups of a smooth threefold along curves explain why such a product is necessary for rationality. A cubic fourfold has vanishing odd cohomology, so its intermediate Jacobians do not contain the corresponding information. Its nontrivial Hodge structure occurs in degree four. Blow-ups along surfaces contribute their degree-two cohomology, which requires a different comparison.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="cfr-scrolls-pfaffians">
    <title>Scrolls, del Pezzo surfaces, and Pfaffian cubics</title>
    <p>Let <m>\Sigma\subset\mathbb P^5</m> be a smooth quartic rational normal scroll. Through a general point of <m>\mathbb P^5</m> there is a unique secant line to <m>\Sigma</m>. If <m>X</m> contains <m>\Sigma</m>, the residual point of intersection of that secant with <m>X</m> parametrizes <m>X</m> by unordered pairs of points of <m>\Sigma</m>. Since the symmetric square of a rational surface is rational, this proves rationality. Equivalently, the quadrics containing <m>\Sigma</m> define a birational map from <m>X</m> to a smooth quadric fourfold. The blow-up of <m>\Sigma</m> resolves the map <xref ref="bib-cfr-fano"/>; a modern proof is given in <xref ref="bib-cfr-brs" detail="Theorem 2.2"/>.</p>
    <p>Morin attempted to deduce rationality of the general cubic from the existence of quartic scrolls <xref ref="bib-cfr-morin" detail="Sections 7–8"/>. The incidence variety of a scroll and a cubic containing it has dimension <m>56</m>, whereas all cubic equations form <m>\mathbb P^{55}</m>. The missing point is the dimension of the family of scrolls inside a fixed cubic. Fano showed that this family has dimension two on the general cubic admitting such scrolls. The image of the incidence variety therefore has dimension <m>54</m>, giving a divisor, now denoted <m>\mathcal C_{14}</m>, rather than the full space of cubics. The secant construction remains valid on that divisor.</p>
    <p>A smooth quintic del Pezzo surface <m>S\subset\mathbb P^5</m>, embedded by its anticanonical system, gives another rationality construction. Its five defining quadrics contract its secant lines. Restricting the resulting map <m>\mathbb P^5\dashrightarrow\mathbb P^4</m> to a cubic containing <m>S</m> gives a birational map: a general contracted secant meets the cubic in its two points on <m>S</m> and one residual point. Tregub studied the relation between this construction and quartic scrolls <xref ref="bib-cfr-tregub"/>.</p>
    <p>A Pfaffian cubic is defined by the Pfaffian of a <m>6\times6</m> skew-symmetric matrix of linear forms. A general point of such a cubic represents a skew form of rank four on a six-dimensional vector space <m>V</m>. Fix a hyperplane <m>W\subset V</m>. The line <m>\ker(\varphi)\cap W</m> gives a point of <m>\mathbb P(W)\simeq\mathbb P^4</m> and defines a birational map from the general Pfaffian cubic. The orthogonal linear section of <m>\operatorname{Gr}(2,V)</m> is a K3 surface of degree <m>14</m>. Beauville and Donagi related the variety of lines of a general Pfaffian cubic to the Hilbert square of this K3 surface <xref ref="bib-cfr-beauville-donagi" detail="Propositions 5–6"/>. This K3 surface explains the Hodge structure appearing in the rationality construction.</p>
    <p>Pfaffian cubics are dense in <m>\mathcal C_{14}</m>. Bolognesi, Russo, and Staglianò proved rationality for every smooth member of <m>\mathcal C_{14}</m> by studying degenerations of quartic scrolls and their secants <xref ref="bib-cfr-brs" detail="Theorems 2.9 and 3.4"/>. The distinction between the divisor and its Pfaffian subset is necessary: the Pfaffian locus is not open in <m>\mathcal C_{14}</m> <xref ref="bib-cfr-brs" detail="Theorem 3.7"/>.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="cfr-plane-secants">
    <title>Projection from a plane and higher secant curves</title>
    <p>If <m>X</m> contains a plane <m>P</m>, projection from <m>P</m> gives a quadric surface fibration
      <md><mrow>q:\operatorname{Bl}_P(X)\longrightarrow\mathbb P^2.</mrow></md>
      Its generic fiber is a quadric over <m>K=\mathbb C(\mathbb P^2)</m>. A rational section gives a <m>K</m>-point, and projection from that point proves that the generic quadric is <m>K</m>-rational. Consequently <m>X</m> is rational. Springer's theorem gives the same conclusion from a multisection of odd degree.</p>
    <theorem xml:id="cfr-plane-criterion">
      <statement><p>Suppose that <m>X</m> contains a plane <m>P</m>, and let <m>h</m> be its hyperplane class. If there is an integral Hodge class <m>\gamma\in H^4(X,\mathbb Z)\cap H^{2,2}(X)</m> such that <m>\gamma\cdot(h^2-[P])</m> is odd, then <m>X</m> is rational.</p></statement>
    </theorem>
    <p>Hassett proves this criterion in <xref ref="bib-cfr-hassett-rational" detail="Theorem 3.1"/>. The class <m>h^2-[P]</m> is the class of a residual quadric. The criterion gives a countable collection of rational loci of codimension two in the moduli space, lying inside the divisor <m>\mathcal C_8</m> of cubics containing a plane <xref ref="bib-cfr-hassett-rational" detail="Theorem 4.2"/>. Two disjoint planes give the simplest section. Tregub's example of a quartic Veronese surface meeting <m>P</m> transversely in three points gives another, since its intersection number with a residual quadric is <m>4-3=1</m>.</p>
    <p>When the sextic discriminant curve is smooth, its double cover is a K3 surface <m>S</m>. The two rulings of the quadrics give a conic bundle over <m>S</m> and a Brauer class <m>\alpha\in\operatorname{Br}(S)[2]</m>. Vanishing of <m>\alpha</m> is equivalent to existence of a rational section of <m>q</m>. This is a sufficient condition for rationality of the total space. It is not necessary: Auel, Bernardara, Bolognesi, and Várilly-Alvarado exhibit rational Pfaffian cubics containing a plane with nonzero Clifford invariant <xref ref="bib-cfr-abbva" detail="Theorem A and Theorem 9"/>.</p>
    <p>Russo and Staglianò extended secant constructions to surfaces admitting a four-dimensional family of five-secant conics, with exactly one such conic through a general point of <m>\mathbb P^5</m>. A cubic containing the surface meets a general conic in six points counted with multiplicity; five lie on the surface, leaving one residual point. Rationality follows when the parameter space of conics is rational. They proved rationality of all smooth cubics in <m>\mathcal C_{26}</m> and <m>\mathcal C_{38}</m> <xref ref="bib-cfr-rs-conics" detail="Theorems 4 and 7"/>. For <m>\mathcal C_{42}</m>, an eight-secant twisted cubic gives the residual point because its intersection degree with a cubic is nine <xref ref="bib-cfr-rs-flops" detail="Theorem 5.12"/>. Their geometric constructions apply to general members. Specialization of rationality in smooth proper families, proved by Kontsevich and Tschinkel, extends the conclusions to every smooth member of these divisors <xref ref="bib-cfr-kontsevich-tschinkel" detail="Theorem 1"/>.</p>
  </section>
</chapter>
